course: dictionary
lesson: 4034
title: Made-up huge numbers
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are not meant to be exact. «I've told you a gazillion times!» does not mean the speaker counted to a gazillion. A gazillion is a made-up, huge number that means «very, very many».
explain: This is called hyperbole: a big exaggeration used to make a point. Gazillion is informal, so you hear it in chatting, stories and jokes, not in a science report.
explain: Gazillion is not a real number like a million or a billion. When you need a real number, count or check. Ask yourself: is the speaker counting, or just saying there are loads?
explain: Other made-up words for loads include zillion, bazillion, squillion and umpteen. «Gazillions of» means an enormous amount, more than anyone could count.
fact: A gazillion is a made-up number, not a real one.
fact: Hyperbole is a big exaggeration used to make a point, not to be taken literally.
fact: «Gazillions of» means an enormous amount, more than anyone could count.
fact: «A gazillion times» means very many times, not an exact count.
fact: Real numbers like million and billion can be counted or checked; gazillion cannot.
exercise: «I've told you a gazillion times!» What does a gazillion mean here? | Exactly one hundred | Only twice | *A made-up, huge number meaning very many | A real number smaller than a million
exercise: «There were gazillions of ants on the picnic.» Which word is the exaggerated number? | There | picnic | stars | *gazillions
exercise: Is gazillion a real number, like a million? | Yes, it is smaller than ten | *No, it is a made-up word for loads | Yes, exactly like a million | Yes, but only used in maths lessons
test: Nan says «I've sent you a gazillion texts.» How could you find out how many she really sent? | Ask the gazillion | Look up gazillion in a maths book | Guess that it is a billion | *Count them on the phone
test: Which sentence uses gazillion as hyperbole, not an exact count? | «The bus leaves at 9 o'clock.» | «She has three pencils.» | «The fifteen sums took about half an hour.» | *«I have asked you a gazillion questions.»
exam: Which is the best explanation of gazillion? | A type of sum that you do in maths | A real number bigger than a billion | *A made-up, huge number used to say very many | A word only used in science reports
